  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1054 AM MDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nCAMs and deterministic models are all indicating a slight \ndowntick in convection this afternoon. This is in response to a\nsomewhat lower moisture content across the region but will not \npreclude convection. Once again, convection will initiate off \nthe higher terrain and drift off in a generally west to east \ndirection. While moisture content has lowered some, we can still\nexpect brief, heavy rain under the stronger and slower moving \ncells with some gusty outflow winds also possible. A few \nlightning strikes have already been noted at this hour so we can\nexpect this convection to continue through the afternoon and \nearly evening hours. We still remain concerned about those \nstronger storms, heavy rainfall rates and burn scars so will \ncontinue to keep an eye on those areas.\n\nBy next week, the high pressure drifts slightly eastward as flow\naround the high will allow another round of deeper moisture to\nmove into the area. Each afternoon and evening will bring new\nrounds of convection with the same concerns noted above. Slow\nmoving storms/showers with heavy rainfall, gusty winds, and\nsmall hail. Those flash flooding concerns remain in place\nespecially near vulnerable terrain and all the burn scars across\nthe area. Wednesday may be the most active day as a weak\nshortwave moves across northern portions of the CWA. This\nincreased lift and instability may make for a more active\nday. May see a downturn in activity after that as the high\npressure drifts directly overhead inhibiting some convection. \nWe shall see. High temperatures will remain on their warm \nstreak...running anywhere from 5 to 10 degrees above more usual late\nJuly values.",
